          A riding lawn mower can last anywhere from five seasons to well over fifteen, and the spread is not luck. Budget tractors with light-duty transmissions and stamped decks tend to wear out at the low end; well-maintained machines with good engines, heavier transmissions and fabricated decks routinely reach the high end. Hours on the engine are the honest measure, and knowing what fails first tells you both what to buy and how to keep it running.
