                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-8 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Budget lawn tractor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8-12 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mid-range tractor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15+ yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Premium or garden tractor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">500-1,500+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Engine hours by class</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="hours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Engine Hours by Class
          </h2>

          <p>
            Engine life in hours depends heavily on design. Entry-level single-cylinder residential engines are commonly expected to deliver somewhere around 500 to 1,000 hours with proper care. Better twin-cylinder residential engines from brands with strong reputations commonly reach 1,000 to 1,500 hours or more, and commercial-grade engines on heavier machines are often expected to exceed 2,000 hours. These are typical expectations, not guarantees.
          </p>

          <p>
            Translate hours into years with your own usage. A lawn that takes one hour a week over a 28-week season adds about 28 hours a year, so a 750-hour engine would theoretically last more than two decades. In practice, other components such as the transmission, the deck and the electrical system usually wear out first, which is why residential riders rarely die of engine hours alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            That changes on larger properties. Mowing four acres for three hours a week adds around 80 to 90 hours a season, and an entry engine can reach its expected range within ten years. Larger properties are exactly where commercial-grade engines, heavier transmissions and fabricated decks pay back their higher cost, because the machine is accumulating hours at several times the suburban rate.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are buying used, the hour meter tells you more than the model year. A ten-year-old machine with 200 hours from a small lot can be a better buy than a four-year-old machine with 500 hours from a large one. Confirm the meter works, since some are mechanical and can stop, and ask for service records to match the hours.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
