        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Riding lawn mowers typically last five to eight seasons at the budget end and fifteen years or more at the premium end, and engine hours are a better measure than years. In residential use the transmission, the deck and the electrical system usually decide when a machine is finished, not the engine.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy a machine with a good engine, a heavier transmission, a fabricated deck and greaseable spindles, then store it under cover, change the oil on schedule, grease the fittings and treat the fuel before winter. That combination is what separates the mower you replace in year six from the one still running in year sixteen.
          </p>
